    <!-- Add Activity -->

  <axiom component="lesson-plan">
      <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>Suggested Time</title>
          <p>15 minutes</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Objective</title>
          <p>To discover the rules for combining surds through grouping like terms involving surds.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Teacher's Role</title>
          <p>Guide learners to compare adding surds to adding algebraic variables. Use a calculator to demonstrate that <m>\sqrt{2} + \sqrt{3} \neq \sqrt{5}</m>.</p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </axiom>

   <activity xml:id="activity-surd-length-discovery">
        <title></title>
        <statement>
            <li>
                <title>Materials Needed</title>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>Square grid paper or stiff cardstock templates.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Scissors and rulers for each pair of learners.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Standard scientific calculators.</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </li>
            <p>
                Working in pairs, you are going to investigate how lengths of irrational cuts behave when combined. Consider three square cards with areas of exactly <m>2\text{ cm}^2</m>, <m>8\text{ cm}^2</m>, and <m>3\text{ cm}^2</m>.
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Recall that the side length of a square is the square root of its area. Write down the exact side length of each square as a surd:
                        <ul>
                            <li><p>Side of Square A (Area = <m>2\text{ cm}^2</m>): <m>\sqrt{2}</m> cm</p></li>
                            <li><p>Side of Square B (Area = <m>8\text{ cm}^2</m>): <m>\sqrt{8}</m> cm</p></li>
                            <li><p>Side of Square C (Area = <m>3\text{ cm}^2</m>): <m>\sqrt{3}</m> cm</p></li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Place Square A and Square B edge-to-edge along a straight line. 
                        <ol label="a">
                            <li><p>Use your calculator to find the total decimal value of <m>\sqrt{2} + \sqrt{8}</m> to 2 decimal places.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Simplify <m>\sqrt{8}</m> into the form <m>a\sqrt{b}</m>. Now add it to <m>1\sqrt{2}</m> like an algebraic expression (treating <m>\sqrt{2}</m> like a variable <m>x</m>). What single surd expression do you get?</p></li>
                            <li><p>Convert your final expression to a decimal. Does it match your measurement from part (a)?</p></li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Now, place Square A and Square C edge-to-edge along a straight line.
                        <ol label="a">
                            <li><p>Find the combined decimal value of their sides (<m>\sqrt{2} + \sqrt{3}</m>) using a calculator.</p></li>
                            <li><p>A student claims that since <m>2 + 3 = 5</m>, the combined length must equal <m>\sqrt{5}</m> cm. Calculate the decimal value of <m>\sqrt{5}</m>. Does it match your actual physical total? Explain why you cannot combine these two surds into one radical sign.</p></li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
    </activity>
